WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.756
Black Adam is a menacing figure not afraid&nbsp;
to kill to deliver his brand of justice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.756 --> 00:00:10.440
But how does Dwayne Johnson compare&nbsp;
to his alter ego as the DC anti-hero?

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.720
The newest DC Extended Universe film, "Black&nbsp;
Adam," was just released to theaters last week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:21.780
on October 21, 2022. Directed by Jaume&nbsp;
Collet-Serra, the film stars Dwayne "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:27.600
Rock" Johnson as the titular Black Adam, a former&nbsp;
slave from Kahndaq who possesses the same powers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.540
as a similar character, Shazam. They include&nbsp;
gifts like super strength and speed, as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:38.760
as more psychological powers such as courage&nbsp;
and wisdom. After being imprisoned for nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.980
5,000 years, Black Adam finally escapes&nbsp;
and is ready to bring justice to the world.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:50.880
"You can be the destroyer of this&nbsp;
world, or you can be its savior."

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.740
While Johnson leads the pack, the film also&nbsp;
boasts an impressive list of supporting cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:00.360
members. This includes Pierce Brosnan&nbsp;
as Doctor Fate, a senior member of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:05.100
Justice Society of America; Aldis&nbsp;
Hodge as Hawkman, the JSA leader;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.920
Noah Centineo as Atom Smasher, a young JSA&nbsp;
member who can control his size and strength;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.560
and Quintessa Swindell as Cyclone, a JSA&nbsp;
member who can control wind and sound. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.780
there is Marwan Kenzari as Sabbac, the militant&nbsp;
leader of the criminal organization Intergang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:28.020
and Sarah Shahi as professor and resistance&nbsp;
fighter Adrianna Tomaz, an ally to the JSA.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:33.360
In a September 2022 interview with Vanity&nbsp;
Fair, Noah Centineo discussed "Black Adam"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.160
and opened up about working with Dwayne&nbsp;
Johnson for the film. Centineo, for one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.860
had nothing but praise for Johnson as an&nbsp;
actor, co-star, and role model. Centineo said,

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.980
"Dwayne [Johnson] is 99 percent more present than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.060
most people. They say don't meet&nbsp;
your idols — not true with Dwayne."

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.860
Centineo also noted that working on "Black&nbsp;
Adam" acted as a learning experience for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.440
as well, thanks in part to Johnson, saying,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:03.240
"Pierce Brosnan is to the right, The Rock&nbsp;
is to the left. I'm getting a master class."

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.960
The actor then spoke again about&nbsp;
working with Johnson in an October&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:12.000
2022 interview with Pinkvilla alongside&nbsp;
his co-star Quintessa Swindell. Both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.740
stars had nothing but praise for Johnson.&nbsp;
Swindell began by noting that he is funnier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.880
than they expected and made everyone&nbsp;
laugh on set constantly, then added,

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.560
"Also, just to work with and be around and&nbsp;
to learn from. And he's incredibly giving."

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.740
Centineo added that one of&nbsp;
the first things he noticed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.760
which Swindell wholeheartedly agreed&nbsp;
with, is that Johnson's social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.300
presence matches up to what he's&nbsp;
like in real life. Centineo said,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.600
"So, I feel like he's very honest&nbsp;
about who he is, which is tight."

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.940
Back in August 2021, Pierce Brosnan was already&nbsp;
beginning to publicly praise Dwayne Johnson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.380
for his work in "Black Adam" while speaking&nbsp;
with Haute Living for an interview. Brosnan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.280
was filming "Black Adam" at the time of the&nbsp;
interview, so his opinions on his co-star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.900
were fresh. Brosnan noted that, due to the&nbsp;
nature of the film, he didn't spend a ton of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:03.180
time on set working with Johnson but that he&nbsp;
has respect for his co-star overall. He said,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.260
"As for Dwayne, I don't know the man,&nbsp;
but I've got great admiration for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.160
and what he's created for himself in life&nbsp;
and how he conducts himself with the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.120
at large. So we go to set and get on with&nbsp;
the work, and that's what you have to do."

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.920
Brosnan then praised Johnson again a month&nbsp;
later while speaking to Collider in September&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.260
2021 to promote 2021's "Cinderella."&nbsp;
Brosnan gave fans some early insight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:30.240
into what to expect from Johnson's superhero&nbsp;
performance. The former James Bond actor said,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.440
"It's an ensemble piece, 'Black Adam.'&nbsp;
You have Dwayne, who's at the pinnacle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:39.720
of his fame and fortune and success and&nbsp;
popularity. He is magnificent as Black Adam."

